{
 "section": "09",
 "slug": "09-03-sacsayhuaman",
 "heading": "## 09 — #3 SACSAYHUAMÁN, PERU",
 "text": "Number three: Sacsayhuamán, the Inca complex above Cusco. Its zigzag walls are built from enormous polygonal stones, the largest with weight estimates ranging from about one hundred to two hundred tonnes, fitted without mortar. One leading explanation for the tight joints is patient repetition: shape a stone, test it against its neighbour, mark where it touches, adjust, and test again. Experiments with hammerstones show how effective that can be. How the largest blocks were moved and lifted is still uncertain. And earthquakes? Interlocking walls have helped many Inca structures survive centuries of tremors, but they aren't indestructible. Much of Sacsayhuamán was dismantled by the Spanish to build colonial Cusco. The biggest stones stayed, too heavy to take. So far, everything went up. Number two goes down.",
 "text_chars": 818,
 "previous_text": "They needed extraordinary skill. Centuries later, the Inca took fitted stone to an extreme.",
 "next_text": "Number two: Derinkuyu, in Cappadocia. Beneath the town lies an underground complex of multiple levels.",
